app AggregateSelfHost:
    # Self-hosting port of facetql/src/core/aggregate.rs — the fold that
    # turns many matching rows into one answer (count/sum/avg/min/max),
    # so a caller never has to ship rows across the wire just to add them
    # up client-side. Read in full before this port: it is ~480 lines of
    # pure reduction math, no I/O, no storage access — AggFunc (which
    # aggregate), AggSpec (function + the field it reads, validated
    # up front), Accumulator (the running fold), and two small JSON-safety
    # helpers (describe, number_from_f64/number_from_i128) it calls at the
    # edges. There is no grouping/bucketing CODE in aggregate.rs itself —
    # its own doc comment mentions "one group whose key is nothing" only
    # to explain why Count lives in the same enum as the rest; a caller
    # wanting one answer per group is expected to run one Accumulator per
    # group (not shown in this file, so not ported here — see the module
    # doc's own scope: "the fold, and nothing else").
    #
    # No crypto anywhere in this file: it is arithmetic over already-
    # decoded values, nothing hashed, nothing signed.
    #
    # ============================================================
    # ROW/VALUE MODEL — reusing predicate.fct's and query.fct's own
    # established shape for a decoded row: one named `Field` per column,
    # `kind` tagging which of num/text/bool/null it holds, `num` carried
    # as `float` throughout (both files document why: this language's
    # `int`/`float` distinction has nothing to attach to on the JSON side,
    # since predicate.rs/engine.rs already run every numeric operand
    # through `as_f64` or an untyped compare). This file's `FieldValue` is
    # that exact same four-field shape (name dropped — a fold reads one
    # already-selected field's value, not a whole row), plus the one case
    # neither sibling file's row-value needs but this one does: "absent"
    # (query.fct's OrderValue already has this for its own reason — a
    # missing `order by` field — and aggregate.rs needs it for the same
    # reason `fold`'s own `value: Option<&Value>` does: a row can simply
    # not have the aggregated field at all).
    #
    # A DELIBERATE, DOCUMENTED SIMPLIFICATION FOLLOWS FROM THIS CHOICE:
    # aggregate.rs's `Accumulator` tracks `int_sum: i128` / `all_int`
    # separately from `float_sum`, specifically so a sum of integer
    # columns renders back out as a JSON integer (`42`, not `42.0`) and
    # survives a total wider than `i64` without precision loss
    # (`an_integer_sum_wider_than_i64_is_still_right`). That distinction
    # depends on knowing whether a JSON number was ORIGINALLY encoded as
    # an integer or a float literal (`serde_json::Value::Number` keeps
    # that tag; `json!(3.0).as_i64()` is `None` even though 3.0 is a whole
    # number) — a distinction the row model here has already erased one
    # layer up, in the very `Field`/`OrderValue` shape predicate.fct and
    # query.fct established: `num` is one `float`, int-origin and
    # float-origin alike, with no int/float tag surviving into it. This
    # port cannot un-erase that tag without inventing a second, richer row
    # shape the task was explicit not to do ("don't invent a second [row
    # value shape]"). What IS ported for real: the actual reduction math —
    # skip null/absent, refuse (not silently coerce) a non-numeric field
    # on sum/avg, divide by the rows that contributed rather than the rows
    # that matched, min/max via the same total order the source's own
    # `compare_order_values` defines. What is deferred, narrowly, is only
    # the JSON-rendering distinction of "does an all-integer sum print
    # without a decimal point" — the numeric ANSWER this port computes is
    # exactly right (`sum([1,2,39]) == 42.0`, numerically identical to the
    # source's `42`); only whether that 42 would serialize with a `.0` is
    # left unaddressed, because there is no wire/JSON encoder in this
    # selfhost slice for either sibling file either. If `Field.kind` ever
    # grows a real "int" vs "num"/float distinction, this is the file to
    # revisit.
    #
    # A SECOND, ALREADY-CONFIRMED SIMPLIFICATION: min/max ordering. The
    # source's `Accumulator::fold` orders MIN/MAX candidates with
    # `keys::compare_order_values` (storage/index.rs) — a total order
    # across JSON types (null < bool < num < text) with a REAL byte-order
    # (`str::cmp`) comparison within two texts, per
    # `min_and_max_order_text_as_the_indexes_do`
    # (`min(["pear","apple","quince"]) == "apple"`). query.fct already
    # confirmed, twice over (its own header, and fabric.fct's
    # `failoverBestCandidateIndex` before it), that `facet lang` has no
    # `ord`/codepoint builtin and no lexicographic `<`/`<=` on `text` —
    # only `==`/`!=`. This port leans on that same confirmed gap rather
    # than re-deriving it: `compareFieldValues`' "text" arm below answers
    # `0` for two texts regardless of their real order (equality is still
    # exact — unequal text just can't be ORDERED). The one place that
    # bites, concretely: MIN/MAX over a text field keeps whichever value
    # was seen FIRST rather than the alphabetically extreme one (`0` never
    # counts as "better" against either `<` or `>`), which is a real,
    # named divergence from `min_and_max_order_text_as_the_indexes_do` —
    # not a silent wrong answer, a documented one. MIN/MAX over `num`
    # (this port's fully-verified case) hits none of this: the source's
    # own numeric compare is already plain `f64::partial_cmp`, identical
    # to this port's float compare, so numeric min/max is an exact port.
    # ============================================================
    struct FieldValue:
        kind: text
        numVal: float
        boolVal: bool
        textVal: text

    proc numValue(v: float) -> FieldValue:
        return FieldValue{kind: "num", numVal: v, boolVal: false, textVal: ""}

    proc textValue(v: text) -> FieldValue:
        return FieldValue{kind: "text", numVal: 0.0, boolVal: false, textVal: v}

    proc boolValue(v: bool) -> FieldValue:
        return FieldValue{kind: "bool", numVal: 0.0, boolVal: v, textVal: ""}

    proc nullValue() -> FieldValue:
        return FieldValue{kind: "null", numVal: 0.0, boolVal: false, textVal: ""}

    proc absentValue() -> FieldValue:
        return FieldValue{kind: "absent", numVal: 0.0, boolVal: false, textVal: ""}

    # ============================================================
    # AggFunc — restated as plain `text` ("count"/"sum"/"avg"/"min"/"max"),
    # matching predicate.fct's own convention of spelling an enum out as
    # its wire text instead of an opaque tag (`node.kind == "bin"`, not
    # `node.kind == 9`). `AggFunc::name()` is then just identity on this
    # representation, so only `parse` (validation) needs its own proc.
    # ============================================================
    struct ParseAggFuncResult:
        ok: bool
        func: text
        err: text

    # parseAggFunc: `AggFunc::parse`'s own body — the wire spelling, or a
    # refusal naming the whole set, verbatim (this port's text
    # concatenation stands in for Rust's `format!`).
    proc parseAggFunc(name: text) -> ParseAggFuncResult:
        if name == "count":
            return ParseAggFuncResult{ok: true, func: "count", err: ""}
        if name == "sum":
            return ParseAggFuncResult{ok: true, func: "sum", err: ""}
        if name == "avg":
            return ParseAggFuncResult{ok: true, func: "avg", err: ""}
        if name == "min":
            return ParseAggFuncResult{ok: true, func: "min", err: ""}
        if name == "max":
            return ParseAggFuncResult{ok: true, func: "max", err: ""}
        let msg = "unknown aggregate \"" + name + "\"; expected one of count, sum, avg, min, max"
        return ParseAggFuncResult{ok: false, func: "", err: msg}

    # aggFuncNeedsField: `AggFunc::needs_field` verbatim — every aggregate
    # but `count` has to read a field out of each row.
    proc aggFuncNeedsField(func: text) -> bool:
        return func != "count"

    # ============================================================
    # AggSpec — the function plus the field it reads (empty/unused for
    # `count`), validated once up front exactly as `AggSpec::new` does:
    # a function that needs a field but got none, or `count` handed a
    # field it would silently ignore, is refused before any row is read.
    # `hasField`/`field` restates `Option<String>` the same way query.fct's
    # `Cursor.hasOrder`/`orderVal` restates its own `Option`.
    # ============================================================
    struct AggSpec:
        func: text
        hasField: bool
        field: text

    struct NewAggSpecResult:
        ok: bool
        err: text
        spec: AggSpec

    proc aggSpecCount() -> AggSpec:
        return AggSpec{func: "count", hasField: false, field: ""}

    # newAggSpec: `AggSpec::new`'s own two refusals, verbatim, then the
    # same `field.filter(|f| !f.is_empty())` normalization on the
    # accepted path.
    proc newAggSpec(func: text, hasField: bool, field: text) -> NewAggSpecResult:
        let needsField = do aggFuncNeedsField(func)
        let realHasField = hasField && field != ""

        if needsField:
            if realHasField == false:
                let msg = func + " needs a field to aggregate; pass `field`"
                return NewAggSpecResult{ok: false, err: msg, spec: AggSpec{func: func, hasField: false, field: ""}}
        else:
            if realHasField:
                let msg2 = "count aggregates rows, not the field \"" + field + "\"; omit `field`, or use a predicate to select the rows to count"
                return NewAggSpecResult{ok: false, err: msg2, spec: AggSpec{func: func, hasField: false, field: ""}}

        let mut finalField = ""
        if realHasField:
            finalField = field
        let spec = AggSpec{func: func, hasField: realHasField, field: finalField}
        return NewAggSpecResult{ok: true, err: "", spec: spec}

    proc aggSpecNeedsField(spec: AggSpec) -> bool:
        let needs = do aggFuncNeedsField(spec.func)
        return needs

    # ============================================================
    # THE TOTAL ORDER — `compare_order_values`, restated over `FieldValue`
    # exactly as query.fct's own `compareOrderValues` restates it over
    # `OrderValue` (same rank numbers, same shape, same text-order
    # scope note — see this file's header for why). Duplicated locally
    # rather than shared: procs/structs don't cross files in this
    # language, matching every sibling selfhost file's own convention.
    # ============================================================
    proc fieldValueTypeRank(v: FieldValue) -> int:
        if v.kind == "null":
            return 10
        if v.kind == "bool":
            return 20
        if v.kind == "num":
            return 30
        if v.kind == "text":
            return 40
        return 90

    proc compareFieldValues(a: FieldValue, b: FieldValue) -> int:
        let ra = do fieldValueTypeRank(a)
        let rb = do fieldValueTypeRank(b)
        if ra < rb:
            return -1
        if ra > rb:
            return 1
        if a.kind == "bool":
            if a.boolVal == b.boolVal:
                return 0
            if a.boolVal:
                return 1
            return -1
        if a.kind == "num":
            if a.numVal < b.numVal:
                return -1
            if a.numVal > b.numVal:
                return 1
            return 0
        if a.kind == "text":
            # No lexicographic order on `text` in this language (confirmed
            # gap — see this file's header). Equal text still compares
            # equal; unequal text also answers 0, a documented
            # simplification rather than a real order.
            return 0
        return 0

    # describeFieldValue: `describe`'s own body, restated over the four
    # kinds a `FieldValue` can actually hold (no array/object case — this
    # row model, like predicate.fct's and query.fct's, has no generic
    # "any JSON value" leaf, so a composite field never reaches here).
    proc describeFieldValue(v: FieldValue) -> text:
        if v.kind == "null":
            return "null"
        if v.kind == "absent":
            return "null"
        if v.kind == "bool":
            return "a boolean"
        if v.kind == "num":
            return "a number"
        if v.kind == "text":
            if len(v.textVal) <= 32:
                return "the text \"" + v.textVal + "\""
            return "text"
        return "a value"

    # ============================================================
    # Accumulator — the running fold. Restated as an immutable value
    # threaded through `foldValue` (which returns the NEXT accumulator
    # rather than mutating in place, since a `proc` parameter is a
    # value here, same convention `sortRowsAscending` uses for `[Row]`),
    # plus `FoldOutcome` standing in for `fold`'s own `Result<(), String>`
    # (this language has no `Result`; `ok`/`err` is the same split
    # predicate.fct's `evalPredicateOk`/`evalPredicateResult` already
    # uses for the identical reason).
    # ============================================================
    struct Accumulator:
        func: text
        rows: int
        contributed: int
        sum: float
        hasExtreme: bool
        extreme: FieldValue

    struct FoldOutcome:
        acc: Accumulator
        ok: bool
        err: text

    proc newAccumulator(func: text) -> Accumulator:
        let av = do absentValue()
        return Accumulator{func: func, rows: 0, contributed: 0, sum: 0.0, hasExtreme: false, extreme: av}

    proc accumulatorRows(acc: Accumulator) -> int:
        return acc.rows

    # foldValue: `Accumulator::fold`'s own body. `rows` increments before
    # anything else — including before a refusal — exactly as the source
    # does (`self.rows += 1` is the first line of `fold`, ahead of the
    # `?`), so a row that FAILED to contribute is still counted as one
    # that was read.
    proc foldValue(acc: Accumulator, field: text, value: FieldValue) -> FoldOutcome:
        let rows2 = acc.rows + 1

        if acc.func == "count":
            let counted = Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: rows2, contributed: acc.contributed, sum: acc.sum, hasExtreme: acc.hasExtreme, extreme: acc.extreme}
            return FoldOutcome{acc: counted, ok: true, err: ""}

        if value.kind == "null":
            let skippedN = Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: rows2, contributed: acc.contributed, sum: acc.sum, hasExtreme: acc.hasExtreme, extreme: acc.extreme}
            return FoldOutcome{acc: skippedN, ok: true, err: ""}

        if value.kind == "absent":
            let skippedA = Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: rows2, contributed: acc.contributed, sum: acc.sum, hasExtreme: acc.hasExtreme, extreme: acc.extreme}
            return FoldOutcome{acc: skippedA, ok: true, err: ""}

        if acc.func == "sum" || acc.func == "avg":
            if value.kind != "num":
                let desc = do describeFieldValue(value)
                let msg = acc.func + " over \"" + field + "\" found " + desc + " in a row that matched; an aggregate over a field that is not always a number has no answer, so this is refused rather than reported as a total over the rows that happened to be numeric"
                let errored = Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: rows2, contributed: acc.contributed, sum: acc.sum, hasExtreme: acc.hasExtreme, extreme: acc.extreme}
                return FoldOutcome{acc: errored, ok: false, err: msg}

            let summed = Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: rows2, contributed: acc.contributed + 1, sum: acc.sum + value.numVal, hasExtreme: acc.hasExtreme, extreme: acc.extreme}
            return FoldOutcome{acc: summed, ok: true, err: ""}

        # min/max: never refuses — `compare_order_values` is a total order
        # across every JSON type, so even a field that mixes types across
        # rows still orders (this is exactly why the source's own MIN/MAX
        # arm has no `as_f64`-style type check, unlike SUM/AVG above).
        let mut better = true
        if acc.hasExtreme:
            let cmp = do compareFieldValues(value, acc.extreme)
            if acc.func == "min":
                better = cmp < 0
            else:
                better = cmp > 0

        let mut nextExtreme = acc.extreme
        let mut nextHasExtreme = acc.hasExtreme
        if better:
            nextExtreme = value
            nextHasExtreme = true

        let extremed = Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: rows2, contributed: acc.contributed + 1, sum: acc.sum, hasExtreme: nextHasExtreme, extreme: nextExtreme}
        return FoldOutcome{acc: extremed, ok: true, err: ""}

    # foldRows: `Accumulator::fold_rows`'s own body — folding `by` rows at
    # once, legal only for `count` (the source's own `debug_assert`; this
    # port makes the same rule a plain runtime no-op for any other
    # function rather than a crash, since a `proc` has no assert/panic to
    # call — a caller passing the wrong function here is a caller bug the
    # same way the source's debug_assert only catches it in a debug
    # build).
    proc foldRows(acc: Accumulator, by: int) -> Accumulator:
        let needs = do aggFuncNeedsField(acc.func)
        if needs:
            return acc
        return Accumulator{func: acc.func, rows: acc.rows + by, contributed: acc.contributed, sum: acc.sum, hasExtreme: acc.hasExtreme, extreme: acc.extreme}

    # isNaNFloat: the one runtime check this port can still make without a
    # dedicated `isNaN`/`isInf` builtin (neither is in `facet lang`'s
    # built-in list) — NaN is the only IEEE-754 value that compares
    # unequal to itself, which is exactly `number_from_f64`'s own trigger
    # for answering `null` instead of a number. Infinity (`number_from_f64`'s
    # OTHER trigger) has no equally cheap test without `isInf`, and isn't
    # reachable by any of this file's own demo/verification data (a finite
    # sum of finite floats), so it is left unhandled rather than faked
    # with an ad hoc magnitude check.
    proc isNaNFloat(x: float) -> bool:
        return x != x

    # finishAccumulator: `Accumulator::finish`'s own body — the empty-case
    # split (count/sum have an identity, avg/min/max are `null`) verbatim,
    # plus the NaN guard `number_from_f64` applies to whichever of
    # sum/avg's answers came out of division or accumulation.
    proc finishAccumulator(acc: Accumulator) -> FieldValue:
        if acc.func == "count":
            let countVal = do numValue(toFloat(acc.rows))
            return countVal

        if acc.func == "sum":
            let sumBad = do isNaNFloat(acc.sum)
            if sumBad:
                let n1 = do nullValue()
                return n1
            let sumVal = do numValue(acc.sum)
            return sumVal

        if acc.func == "avg":
            if acc.contributed == 0:
                let n2 = do nullValue()
                return n2
            let avg = acc.sum / toFloat(acc.contributed)
            let avgBad = do isNaNFloat(avg)
            if avgBad:
                let n3 = do nullValue()
                return n3
            let avgVal = do numValue(avg)
            return avgVal

        # min/max
        if acc.hasExtreme:
            return acc.extreme
        let n4 = do nullValue()
        return n4

    # ============================================================
    # THE ENTRY POINT — the calling convention every one of
    # aggregate.rs's own callers (not shown in this file — they live in
    # `StorageEngine`) follows: build an `Accumulator`, fold every
    # matching row's value in, stop at the first refusal exactly as a
    # `for row in rows { acc.fold(...)?; }` loop's `?` would, and finish.
    # This is not new logic beyond aggregate.rs — it is the loop-and-
    # finish shape `Accumulator`'s own doc comments describe throughout —
    # restated here as one proc so this file, like predicate.fct's
    # `evalPredicateOk`/`evalPredicateResult` and query.fct's
    # `computeQueryPage`, has a single top-level call a future
    # self-hosted scan loop can actually reach for.
    # ============================================================
    struct AggResult:
        ok: bool
        err: text
        result: FieldValue
        rows: int

    proc aggregateOverValues(func: text, field: text, values: [FieldValue]) -> AggResult:
        let acc0 = do newAccumulator(func)
        let mut acc = acc0
        let n = len(values)
        let mut i = 0
        let mut failed = false
        let mut errMsg = ""
        loop i < n:
            if failed == false:
                let outcome = do foldValue(acc, field, values[i])
                acc = outcome.acc
                if outcome.ok == false:
                    failed = true
                    errMsg = outcome.err
            i = i + 1

        if failed:
            let failResult = do nullValue()
            return AggResult{ok: false, err: errMsg, result: failResult, rows: acc.rows}

        let finished = do finishAccumulator(acc)
        return AggResult{ok: true, err: "", result: finished, rows: acc.rows}

    # ============================================================
    # DEMO / BUILD-CHECK FIXTURE — each proc below hand-traces one of
    # aggregate.rs's own `#[test]`s, by name in the comment, so a
    # divergence from the real Rust value (there is exactly one,
    # documented at this file's header: MIN/MAX over text) is visible
    # at the call site rather than only in prose.
    # ============================================================
    proc numsFromFloats3(a: float, b: float, c: float) -> [FieldValue]:
        let va = do numValue(a)
        let vb = do numValue(b)
        let vc = do numValue(c)
        return [va, vb, vc]

    # a_sum_of_integers_stays_an_integer: sum([1,2,39]) == 42 (numerically
    # exact; see header for the int-vs-float rendering deferral).
    proc demoSumIntegers() -> text:
        let vals = do numsFromFloats3(1.0, 2.0, 39.0)
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("sum", "f", vals)
        return "" + r.result.numVal

    # one_float_makes_the_whole_sum_a_float: sum([1, 0.5]) == 1.5.
    proc demoSumMixed() -> text:
        let v1 = do numValue(1.0)
        let v2 = do numValue(0.5)
        let vals = [v1, v2]
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("sum", "f", vals)
        return "" + r.result.numVal

    # the_empty_sum_is_zero_and_the_empty_average_is_null, plus min/max/count.
    proc demoEmptySum() -> text:
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("sum", "f", [])
        return "" + r.result.numVal

    proc demoEmptyAvgIsNull() -> bool:
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("avg", "f", [])
        return r.result.kind == "null"

    proc demoEmptyMinIsNull() -> bool:
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("min", "f", [])
        return r.result.kind == "null"

    proc demoEmptyCount() -> text:
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("count", "f", [])
        return "" + r.result.numVal

    # an_average_divides_by_the_rows_that_had_a_value: rows 2,4,absent,null
    # -> avg == 3.0 (not 1.5 — the two valueless rows are not zeroes).
    proc demoAvgSkipsMissing() -> text:
        let v1 = do numValue(2.0)
        let v2 = do numValue(4.0)
        let v3 = do absentValue()
        let v4 = do nullValue()
        let vals = [v1, v2, v3, v4]
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("avg", "f", vals)
        return "" + r.result.numVal

    # count_counts_rows_whatever_the_field_holds: absent/null/text all
    # still count as rows -> rows == 3.
    proc demoCountCountsEverything() -> int:
        let v1 = do absentValue()
        let v2 = do nullValue()
        let v3 = do textValue("text")
        let vals = [v1, v2, v3]
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("count", "f", vals)
        return r.rows

    # a_sum_over_text_is_refused_rather_than_skipped: sum([1, "12"]) fails,
    # and the message names both the function and the offending value.
    proc demoSumOverTextRefused() -> bool:
        let v1 = do numValue(1.0)
        let v2 = do textValue("12")
        let vals = [v1, v2]
        let r = do aggregateOverValues("sum", "price", vals)
        if r.ok:
            return false
        let mentionsSum = contains(r.err, "sum")
        let mentionsValue = contains(r.err, "\"12\"")
        return mentionsSum && mentionsValue

    # min_and_max_order_text_as_the_indexes_do — real Rust: min == "apple",
    # max == "quince". This port's DOCUMENTED divergence (see header): no
    # lexicographic order on text, so both min and max keep the FIRST
    # value seen, "pear". Traced explicitly, not silently wrong.
    proc demoMinMaxOverTextFirstSeen() -> text:
        let v1 = do textValue("pear")
        let v2 = do textValue("apple")
        let v3 = do textValue("quince")
        let vals = [v1, v2, v3]
        let minR = do aggregateOverValues("min", "f", vals)
        let maxR = do aggregateOverValues("max", "f", vals)
        return minR.result.textVal + "/" + maxR.result.textVal

    # min/max over NUM is a fully-verified, exact port: real order.
    proc demoMinMaxOverNumbers() -> text:
        let v1 = do numValue(30.0)
        let v2 = do numValue(10.0)
        let v3 = do numValue(20.0)
        let vals = [v1, v2, v3]
        let minR = do aggregateOverValues("min", "f", vals)
        let maxR = do aggregateOverValues("max", "f", vals)
        return "" + minR.result.numVal + "/" + maxR.result.numVal

    # a_spec_refuses_a_function_field_pair_it_cannot_answer.
    proc demoSpecValidation() -> bool:
        let sumNoField = do newAggSpec("sum", false, "")
        let sumEmptyField = do newAggSpec("sum", true, "")
        let countWithField = do newAggSpec("count", true, "price")
        let countOk = do newAggSpec("count", false, "")
        let avgOk = do newAggSpec("avg", true, "price")
        if sumNoField.ok:
            return false
        if sumEmptyField.ok:
            return false
        if countWithField.ok:
            return false
        if countOk.ok == false:
            return false
        if avgOk.ok == false:
            return false
        return true

    # an_unknown_function_names_the_ones_that_exist.
    proc demoParseUnknown() -> bool:
        let r = do parseAggFunc("mean")
        if r.ok:
            return false
        return contains(r.err, "avg")

    state demoSumIntegersResult: text = ""
    state demoSumMixedResult: text = ""
    state demoEmptySumResult: text = ""
    state demoEmptyAvgIsNullResult: bool = false
    state demoEmptyMinIsNullResult: bool = false
    state demoEmptyCountResult: text = ""
    state demoAvgSkipsMissingResult: text = ""
    state demoCountCountsEverythingResult: int = 0
    state demoSumOverTextRefusedResult: bool = false
    state demoMinMaxOverTextResult: text = ""
    state demoMinMaxOverNumbersResult: text = ""
    state demoSpecValidationResult: bool = false
    state demoParseUnknownResult: bool = false

    action runDemoAggregate():
        let a1 = do demoSumIntegers()
        let a2 = do demoSumMixed()
        let a3 = do demoEmptySum()
        let a4 = do demoEmptyAvgIsNull()
        let a5 = do demoEmptyMinIsNull()
        let a6 = do demoEmptyCount()
        let a7 = do demoAvgSkipsMissing()
        let a8 = do demoCountCountsEverything()
        let a9 = do demoSumOverTextRefused()
        let a10 = do demoMinMaxOverTextFirstSeen()
        let a11 = do demoMinMaxOverNumbers()
        let a12 = do demoSpecValidation()
        let a13 = do demoParseUnknown()
        demoSumIntegersResult = a1
        demoSumMixedResult = a2
        demoEmptySumResult = a3
        demoEmptyAvgIsNullResult = a4
        demoEmptyMinIsNullResult = a5
        demoEmptyCountResult = a6
        demoAvgSkipsMissingResult = a7
        demoCountCountsEverythingResult = a8
        demoSumOverTextRefusedResult = a9
        demoMinMaxOverTextResult = a10
        demoMinMaxOverNumbersResult = a11
        demoSpecValidationResult = a12
        demoParseUnknownResult = a13

    view AggregateHome at "/":
        box:
            text "{demoSumIntegersResult} {demoSumMixedResult} {demoEmptySumResult} {demoEmptyAvgIsNullResult} {demoEmptyMinIsNullResult} {demoEmptyCountResult} {demoAvgSkipsMissingResult} {demoCountCountsEverythingResult} {demoSumOverTextRefusedResult} {demoMinMaxOverTextResult} {demoMinMaxOverNumbersResult} {demoSpecValidationResult} {demoParseUnknownResult}"
